(project borge-lib-inbox
 (doc "Unified queue for scope concerns, bugs, drift findings, review findings. lib/inbox/")
 (status implemented)
 (no-inline
 )
 (section inbox
  (doc "Unified filing queue: .borge-inbox files, one format, many sources")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Implements the inbox subsystem spec'd in docs/agent.borg
        subsections inbox-file-format, inbox-commands, todo-aggregation.

        The inbox is a unified queue: scope concerns (filed during
        implementation), bug reports, drift findings, review findings
        all flow into the same .borge-inbox file format. Replaces the
        narrower bug subsystem (lib/bug/) — the old `borge bug` commands
        become deprecated aliases for `borge inbox file --source bug-report`.

        One queue, one format, tracked in git. The inbox is the outlet
        the borge-workflow gate creates: agents in Execute mode CAN'T
        edit .borg files, so they file inbox items for spec concerns
        instead. Humans triage in Plan mode.

        Format (per docs/agent.borg inbox-file-format):
          (borge-inbox id:"INBOX-7"
            (title "Need error handling in db_sql")
            (status triage)
            (filed-by agent:implementer)
            (source scope-concern)
            (affects section:"lib.borg:db-sql")
            (created 2026-05-31)
            (doc (|
              During implementation of db_sql.ml, discovered that
              the spec doesn't cover connection retry logic.
            |))
          )

        Status workflow: triage → acknowledged → plan-approved → closed.
        Source variants: scope-concern, bug-report, drift-finding,
        review-finding. Resolution on close: spec-updated, wontfix,
        duplicate, fixed-in-code.

        Three modules:
        1. inbox.ml: types + parse + format + list/file/close/acknowledge/
           approve/consolidate. Pure data, no I/O side effects beyond
           file reads/writes via File_utils.
        2. (CLI lives in bin/cmd/Inbox.ml, not this library)

        What this module does NOT do:
          - Enforce gating. The phase engine (pi/borge-workflow skill)
            enforces "can't edit .borg in Execute mode". This module
            just provides the filing mechanism. Separation of concerns:
            the tool doesn't police itself.
          - Auto-file from drift/review. Drift and review findings CAN
            call Inbox.file to create items, but that wiring lives in
            the drift/review commands, not here.
      |))
  )
  (subsection inbox-ast
   (doc "Inbox item types: status workflow, source variants, resolution")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         inbox_status: triage | acknowledged | plan-approved | closed
         inbox_source: scope-concern | bug-report | drift-finding | review-finding
         inbox_resolution (on close): spec-updated | wontfix | duplicate | fixed-in-code
         inbox_item: { id; title; status; filed_by; source; affects;
                       created; doc; resolution }

         ID format: INBOX-N where N is the next sequential integer
         after scanning existing .borge-inbox files for the max.
         (Bug subsystem used BORGE-{timestamp}; inbox uses sequential
         INBOX-N for human-readability and stable ordering.)
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection inbox-parse
   (doc "Parse .borge-inbox files into typed records")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
        inbox_parse.ml: reads .borge-inbox files (sexp format) into
        inbox_item records using Borge_lang.Parse. Handles the
        id:"INBOX-N" header attribute, the (doc (|...|)) verbatim
        block, and optional (affects section:"...") and (resolution ...)
        sub-forms.

        Robust to missing optional fields: an item with no (affects ...)
        parses with affects=None, etc. Does NOT crash on a partially-
        written item.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection inbox-write
   (doc "Write inbox_item records to .borge-inbox file format")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         inbox_write.ml (or inline in inbox.ml): renders an inbox_item
        to the .borge-inbox sexp format. Idempotent + deterministic
        (fields in canonical order) for clean git diffs, mirroring
        lib/check/meta.ml and bug_write.ml.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection inbox-registry
   (doc "Manage .borge-inbox directory: paths, listing, loading, saving")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         inbox_registry.ml: handles the .borge-inbox/ directory (or
        .borge-inbox files — spec says files, plural, in the repo root
        or a dedicated dir). Mirrors lib/bug/bug_registry.ml:
        - save_item: write to inbox/<ID>.borge-inbox, return path
        - load_item: read by ID
        - list_items: scan dir, return all items (optionally filtered
          by status or source)
        - next_id: scan existing files for max N, return INBOX-(N+1)

        Files live in .borge-inbox/ directory (git-tracked alongside
        code and specs, per the spec).
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection inbox-consolidate
   (doc "Aggregate TODO markers across .borg and .ml files into inbox items")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The `borge inbox consolidate` command. Scans .borg and .ml
        files for TODO/FIXME/XXX markers and (status planned) sections
        with no (details ...), files each as a scope-concern inbox item.
        Deduplicates against existing items (by title similarity).

        Also surfaces (status planned) sections with no details as
        "spec incomplete" scope-concerns.

        This is the dogfooding path: the inbox tool uses itself to
        track its own incomplete specs.
       |))
   )
  )
 )
)
